  "rules": [
    "When history shows a prolonged downtrend (e.g., Example 1-3) followed by a model forecast that assumes immediate recovery or linear growth, apply a mean-reversion correction factor of -5% to -10% because true outcomes often continue falling for several days before rising.",
    "If sentiment history contains a single negative spike (-1.00) immediately preceding the forecast horizon, reduce the model's upward bias by 3-5 points as this signals temporary market stress that forecasts frequently underestimate.",
    "When history exhibits high volatility with recent spikes (e.g., Example 4-5), correct for 'overshoot lag' by reducing the model's projected growth rate by 15-20% since true outcomes often peak later than predicted or fail to sustain immediate momentum.",
    "In volatile regimes, ignore sentiment scores > 0.50 if they appear in isolation; instead, weight recent price action variance higher, as sentiment often lags behind actual price reversals.",
    "Apply a 'double-mean' correction: when the model forecast is significantly above the historical average (e.g., >15% deviation), assume the true outcome will revert to the 30-day moving average within the first 7 days of the forecast horizon."
  ]
}
